### Plain-English summary

This agreement appoints O’Shaughnessy & Sons as a **non-exclusive distributor** of Dunleavy’s food products. Dunleavy may appoint other distributors and sell directly to customers. The stated territory is unclear because it refers to the “Republic of Ireland” but also “all 32 counties.”

The agreement runs from **1 April 2026 to 31 March 2027**. It then renews automatically for further 12-month periods unless either party gives at least **60 days’ written notice** before the current term ends. Either party may terminate for serious breach, insolvency, or late payment, subject generally to notice and cure periods.

O’Shaughnessy must:

- purchase at least **150 cases and €18,000 of products each month**;
- pay invoices within 30 days;
- pay a shortfall charge if minimum purchases are missed, unless waived for force majeure or extraordinary market conditions;
- provide monthly sales and inventory reports and permit quarterly audits;
- maintain public liability insurance of at least €250,000 per claim;
- protect confidential information for five years after termination; and
- follow Dunleavy’s branding and intellectual-property rules.

Dunleavy must meet food-safety and quality requirements, maintain product liability insurance, and replace, repair, or refund defective goods. Prices are fixed until 30 September 2026, after which increases require 30 days’ notice and are capped at 8% in any 12 months.

**Key risks:** automatic renewal; significant minimum-purchase penalties; 8% interest on payment defaults; restricted damages claims; and inconsistent liability wording—one clause says food-safety liability is unlimited, while another imposes a general cap without clearly excluding those claims. These inconsistencies should be clarified in writing.

